WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.829 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  my grandma  loved collecting all these jugs and jars  in our tiny home  this was back in shanghai where i was  born and our family of five  was living in a 550 square feet  apartment  despite the physical limits grandma  would exhaust  all the algorithms to optimize every  single ancient for shelf spades  so that all these containers could  somehow snuggle  in our kitchen cabinet this is a picture  taken at my fifth birthday and you can  see just about  five percent of our total kitchen  collections right there in the top left  corner  we would then use these jars and jugs to  go get freshly ground soy milk from a  local grocery store  or use them to hold spices and tea  leaves and seasoning  all year round when asked  why don't we throw them away grandma she  always smiles back at me and says  there is no away and everything  has a home  now i think my grandma wanted the  five-year-old me to know  that it's a policy scheme to think  everything we throw away would just  magically vanish  we have to be mindful of our waste  footprint on the planet  because after all it is our only home  now my grandparents lived through  poverty in china in 1930s  and they had to live their life under a  conservation mindset  because of economic constraints  we live in a very different time now but  her words  there is no away stuck with me  i'm here today to talk trash because i  believe  her mindfulness of waste should be a  value that we preserve  across generations and geographies  fast forward to today i feel like i'm  disappointing grandma  every time i get an amazon package so i  ordered this for my friends couple weeks  ago  and this tiny thing came in a huge box  with these bubble wraps  now raise your hand if you ever felt the  same way  i know a lot of you do and you're not  alone  this is to show you that i too fall prey  to the modern consumption model and in  fact  one million single-use bottles are  consumed globally every minute  that's enough to fill up an  olympic-sized swimming pool every two  minutes  more than 80 percent of these plastic  waste  are just burned or landfilled because of  a mix  of misaligned incentives lack of  education and insufficient  infrastructure  for material recovery  under current rate of consumption there  will be more plastics than fish in the  ocean by 2050  and the story doesn't end there once  exposed  plastics degrade captioning not  available we're literally  changing the way earth looks and there's  no such thing  as a way a waste garbage patch in the  middle pacific  awaits gilroy california 50 miles from  here  away as the landfill sites next to the  lowest income population in indonesia  and away is the food water  and air that we so depend on every  single day  so now you may be wondering i know this  is bad but  what should we do to change the course  waste is a design flaw and we should  work together  as a society to correct the flaw  consumers businesses governments and  non-profits  we each have a role to play in this team  sport  as business leaders we're uniquely  positioned and empowered  to make skillable impact by creating our  brands into agents of change  so i'm going to focus today's talk on  three levers  that business leaders can use to design  out waste  across the value chain lengthen product  lifecycle  increase operational efficiency and  extend producer responsibility  and the good news is designing out waste  can translate into short-term and  long-term economic gains  let me tell you how first  the post-pandemic world presents an  opportunity for businesses  to shift our economy away from take make  waste let's not make this crisis go to  waste  out of the pandemic consumers will be  savvier than ever  by choosing quality over quantity  functionality over disposability  and value over price  to appeal to this shifting demand  towards  value-based consumption businesses can  look into investing materials  and products that are inherently more  durable and recyclable by nature  in addition we can create incentives and  mechanisms  to engage our consumers in reusing and  recycling the products  by lengthening that product life cycle  we're adding more touch points  along the customer journey and thereby  strengthening that relationship  and building brand loyalty  casing point patagonia successfully  launched the campaign  called don't buy this jacket for black  friday to raise awareness about buying  fewer but better  they also made repair the new fashion  through their one word program  that collects second-hand clothes and  gears  second waste to some extent  means loss of efficiencies in the value  chain and missed opportunities for cost  savings  this is why it makes most direct  economic sense to design  out waste for example  food waste is a 400 billion dollar loss  globally  a company called afresh is targeting the  food waste problem  in the perimeter of grocery stores they  use ai power technologies  to optimize the quantity of fresh foods  ordered every day into the store  as a result they've saved up to 20  million pounds  of food waste and helped grocery stores  increase gross margin in produce by 40  percent  lastly just like any externality  problems  there is a way to use market force to  correct the flaw  and in our case these measures are  called extended  producer responsibility laws or epr  in the u.s california is actually  writing a ballot  to tax producers of single-use plastics  business leaders should proactively  embed these epr  measures into our core business strategy  because again  social norms are changing and  regulations are catching up  doing nothing is no longer a viable  option  here's an example of how companies are  doing it  a small number of environmentally  conscious beverage companies in the bay  area  such as straus and la vie are committed  to collecting back their bottles for  reuse  these programs are core to their mission  to reduce  waste footprint of their products from  end to end  last year more than 50 of the largest  consumer package goods companies  signed the us plastics pact to commit  to a hundred percent reusable recyclable  and compostable packaging by 2025.  it's a step in the right direction but  we need a lot more investment in access  education technology and infrastructure  to make it a reality  as business leaders i want us to  challenge ourselves  to reinvent the business model by  lengthening product life cycle  increasing operational efficiency and  extending producer responsibility at the  end of the day  what should matter to all of us is that  we leave the world a better place than  we found it  a place where waste is no longer a  design flaw  a place where we can proudly tell our  grandkids  there is no away and everything  has a home thank you  you

